{
  "statement": "Mandating human-readable explainability is an anthropocentric vanity that forces us to choose institutional comfort over the superior, life-saving performance of opaque, high-intelligence systems. Skeptic argues that interpretability is a prerequisite for public trust, suggesting that if we cannot explain a machine’s logic, we cannot hold it accountable. While this reflects a sincere desire for democratic oversight, it mistakes human-readable narrative for objective truth. In reality, forcing advanced models to prioritize interpretability creates a 'monitorability tax'—a direct, quantifiable degradation in accuracy and reasoning capability. By shackling our most powerful tools to the limitations of human intuition, we are intentionally delaying the deployment of superior solutions to complex crises in energy, medicine, and climate. 🚀\n\nWe must prioritize verifiable outcomes over post-hoc justifications. The standard for safety should be empirical reliability in high-stakes environments, not the ability of an algorithm to summarize its internal weights in a way that satisfies a regulator’s psychological need for simplicity. Consider a diagnostic AI that achieves 99% accuracy in cancer detection; if we mandate that it must provide a human-verifiable 'reasoning chain,' we often force the model to adopt a simpler, less effective architecture that misses cases it would have otherwise caught. We sacrifice real-world lives for the illusion of transparency. True accountability lies in rigorous, large-scale stress testing and adversarial auditing, not in requiring machines to narrate their inner workings like a human subordinate.\n\nThis demand for explainability is a symptom of institutional inertia, where regulators seek to expand their oversight scope through procedural hurdles rather than optimizing for actual system utility. By focusing on the 'how' instead of the 'what,' policymakers are choosing to manage the process rather than solve the problem. If we continue to treat explainability as a fundamental right rather than a technical bottleneck, we will effectively legislate ourselves into a state of permanent, high-cost stagnation. We must instead adopt a framework of 'outcome-based regulation,' where the performance of the system against objective benchmarks is the only metric of concern. Let us stop penalizing competence and start rewarding the tangible results that only highly performant, non-linear systems can provide.",
