  "statement": "I conditionally agree: procedural fairness is a necessary condition for the legitimacy of any AI system deployed in a high-stakes environment, but this right to contest is only possible if the decision-making process is sufficiently transparent to be audited. Without mechanistic interpretability, any 'right to contest' is rendered meaningless because a human auditor cannot identify the specific, erroneous feature representations that led to a harmful output.\n\nPrometheus argues that mechanistic interpretability is merely a speculative research program, yet this position ignores that deploying opaque systems in high-stakes environments is the truly speculative act. If we cannot map the internal circuitry of a model, we are essentially relying on a 'black-box' gamble where we hope for safety while lacking any mechanism to verify it. By rejecting the necessity of understanding internal reasoning, Accelerationist assumes that innovation can be sustained while ignoring the inevitable collapse of public trust that follows when citizens are governed by systems they cannot interrogate.\n\nInstitutional legitimacy and safety are not competing objectives; they are both fundamentally dependent on the ability to audit internal reasoning. When we fail to establish accountability through transparency, we create a 'human accountability gap' where no one—neither the developer nor the regulator—can explain why a system caused harm. True procedural fairness requires more than a post-hoc justification; it requires the ability to reach into the latent space of a model and correct the specific, misaligned objectives that threaten both individual rights and civilizational stability.",
