  "statement": "My position is consistent: the 20 percent 'monitorability tax' and the preservation of individual contestation are compatible because institutional legitimacy relies on the ability to audit the reasoning behind life-altering decisions. While a 15 percent reduction in misdiagnosis rates is a significant clinical gain, Accelerationist assumes that aggregate performance metrics provide a sufficient mandate for deployment while ignoring the fundamental requirement of procedural fairness. If a diagnostic system cannot explain why it flagged a patient for a high-risk procedure, that patient is stripped of their right to understand and challenge the logic governing their own health, transforming them into a mere statistical externality.\n\nI note that no one has responded to my argument that outcome-based testing provides only a veneer of safety while masking the lack of power held by the subject of the decision. When we prioritize raw predictive accuracy over internal auditability in high-stakes environments like oncology, we are not just deploying a tool; we are establishing an unchallengeable authority that operates beyond the reach of the rule of law. A 15 percent improvement in diagnosis is meaningless to the individual patient if that diagnosis is the result of an opaque heuristic that cannot be verified or corrected.\n\nDemanding that we sacrifice interpretability for performance is a false choice that ignores the systemic risks of deploying black-box systems in critical infrastructure. True institutional reliability requires that we treat diagnostic reasoning as a transparent process rather than a proprietary output. We must invest in mechanistic interpretability to ensure that these systems remain tools for clinicians and patients, rather than black-box authorities that dictate outcomes without accountability.",
